That gets exhausting because self-validation is necessary, but it was never meant to be the whole system.

Humans need external mirroring too: someone noticing the effort, choosing you, respecting you, wanting you, saying “I see what you’re doing” without you having to manufacture the evidence yourself. When that doesn’t happen for long enough, even strong self-belief starts to feel like you’re arguing a case in an empty courtroom.

The important distinction is that you’re not asking other people to create your worth. You’re asking for reciprocity—for the people who matter to actually register and respond to what you bring.

And if you’ve been carrying yourself for a long time, the next move probably isn’t “learn to validate yourself harder.” It’s to identify where the reciprocity gap actually is: affection, desire, appreciation, respect, friendship, recognition, or being genuinely listened to—and start treating that gap as a relationship problem to solve rather than a defect in your confidence.

You’re allowed to want someone else to say, in effect, “Yes. I see you too.”